> What I AM interested in, especially from people who have played Nethack
> for some time, is your thoughts on what would be your ideal control on
> a handheld device like GP32 with 8-way directional and 6 buttons?

Well, I've played Nethack for a long time.  And, as it happens, I have
written a roguelike for a system with an 8 way directional and 6
buttons.  So, I already have my answers to these questions.

The design and play of POWDER has been built to fit within these
limitations.  You have a bit of a difficulty with Nethack as it's
gameplay is already balanced for keyboard/screen interaction (which is
why I didn't bother porting Nethack, but instead focused on making
something new)

First, I'd recommend playing POWDER.  Ideally on actual hardware as it
is hard to appreciate the UI in an emulator.  You should also play AGB
Rogue which has some good ideas, but also shows what you should not do.
 I'd also recommend playing some console RPGs.  Zelda is an example of
a well defined interface.  POWDER owes a lot to figuring out what works
well in Zelda.  (Zelda has remappable buttons, for example, except
rather than binding "B" to "Jump" you instead equip your feather in
your B slot)

To make the game fun, you want to look at common actions and minimize
the button presses required to perform them.

My thoughts:

1) 8 way direction buttons are often not really 8 way direction
buttons.  It is hard to get a clear diagonal response without first
getting a horizontal/vertical response.  Since you *want* keyrepeat for
movement, this can result in much difficulty doing precision diagonal
movement.  I solved this in POWDER by removing diagonal movement from
the game.  This is not an option for Nethack.  The best solution I've
seen would be what was used with AGB Rogue.  Have a modifier button
that triggers the 8-way movement.  Normally the control pad will move
only in 4 ways (horizontal and vertical) but if the modifier is
pressed, it will move only in 4 ways (the diagonals).  This lets the
user confidentally step exactly on a diagonal - essential in those life
and death situations when one is dodging around monsters.

2) Remappable buttons.  Pressing Start brings up a list of all
available commands.  You can either execute it directly (with select)
or bind it to one of the four mappable buttons (a, b, l, r) by pressing
the relevant button.  Then you may execute the command directly.  While
I usually have my mappings fixed (A-Zap, B-Fire, R-Map) some vary with
equipment (L is often Jump or Search) or situation.

3) Auto-prompt.  I disagreed with this when it was first suggested.
However, I have come to love it.  Many things have obvious actions when
you step over them.  By prompting with a menu, you can avoid the need
to bind a button for that task.  The two ones in POWDER are to Climb
ladders and to Pick Up items.  In both cases, [B] will dismiss the menu
and [A] make a selection.  The annoyance of having to dismiss the menu
when walking over items is more than ameliorated by the advantages of
getting to see a full list  of items (rather than "there are many items
here") and, as importantly, freeing up two buttons.

4) Mini-map.  One thing that always killed playing roguelikes on the
small screen for me was that the game was balanced with being able to
see the full screen at once.  Nethack is particularly bad at this,
IMHO.  A common tactics is blindfold + telepathy, and to get the same
effect one needs to constantly scroll around.  For POWDER, I instead
decided to balance it so I wouldn't miss the ability to see everything
at once.  This leaves the issue of finding where you are on a level.
For this, I recommend a mini-map that can be brought up instantly while
a button is pressed.

5) Noun-Verb Inventory.

One of the most contentious changes to enter a limitted button world is
to switch from the verb-heavy approach to a noun-heavy one.  Instead of
selecting "Zap Wand" and then choosing the wand, one reverses it.  One
chooses a Wand and then selects Zap.  All of your inventory related
commands can be compressed into a single command: 'i' - the command
that brings up a screen displaying your current inventory.  One can
select an item, get a list of verbs to apply to it (with the most
logical defaulted), and then use it.

6) 2D Menus.

You can select things with fewer keypresses if they are placed in a 2d
array rather than as a single list.  POWDER only uses this for the
inventory, but it is a point to keep in mind.

> How
> about if the device was capable of touchscreen like a PalmOS device?

CalcRogue is a good place to look for some thoughts there.  I believe
he looked into doing a more gestural input - a pleasant change from
most PalmOS ports I see which are functionally painful to use.

I have dreams of some day moving POWDER to the DS.

The touch screen would be superior in many aspects of roguelike play -
menu selection, casting spells, selecting targets for walk commands,
etc.  Unfortunately, they seem at first glance to be less effective for
melee combat.  Note that a hybrid system is likely unwise - I'd
recommend going full touch-screen rather than demand the user switch
between the stylus and keypad.

> Ultimately I'd like to add more support to nethack for small screen  /
